difficult in the past. \n\nAutism was not well-known or understood until relatively recently. The term "autism" was first used in the 1940s by Hans Asperger and Leo Kanner, but it wasn\'t until much later that it gained wider recognition. This meant fewer people, including medical professionals, were aware of the signs and symptoms.\n\nThe diagnostic criteria for autism have changed over time. Early definitions were much narrower, often focusing more on classic symptoms like severe social withdrawal or repetitive behaviors. Over the years, as understanding of the spectrum broadened, the criteria expanded to include a wider range of behaviors and functionalities, which has made diagnosis more inclusive but also more complex.\n\nBefore autism was well understood, individuals were diagnosed with other conditions like childhood schizophrenia, intellectual disability, or even behavioral issues. Autism shares symptoms with many other disorders, and without specific diagnostic tools, it was easy to mislabel.\n\nIn the past, there were fewer standardized tools for assessing autism. Now, there are numerous validated screening tools like the Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule (ADOS) and the Autism Diagnostic Interview-Revised (ADI-R), which help in making more accurate diagnoses. Early tools were less refined.\n\nSocietal perceptions of disability and neurodiversity have shifted. Previously, behaviors now associated with autism might have been seen simply as "eccentricity" or "bad behavior" rather than part of a neurodevelopmental disorder. Cultural attitudes towards seeking medical help for behavioral issues also played a role in how or whether autism was diagnosed.\n\nDiagnosis often requires access to child psychologists, developmental pediatricians, or neurologists with expertise in autism. In earlier times, or in areas with fewer resources, access to such specialists was more limited, delaying or preventing diagnosis.\n\nOngoing research continues to refine our understanding of autism, including its genetic and environmental factors, which were less understood decades ago. This has led to better diagnostic practices over time.', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 8, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Fri Jan 31 07:33:53 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1885016457949413685', 'display_text_range': [14, 293], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '69156861', 'name': 'Chief Nerd', 'screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'indices': [0, 13]}]}, 'favorite_count': 60, 'favorited': False, 'full_text': '@TheChiefNerd Diagnosing autism was more difficult in the past. \n\nAutism was not well-known or understood until relatively recently. The term "autism" was first used in the 1940s by Hans Asperger and Leo Kanner, but it wasn\'t until much later that it gained wider recognition. This meant fewer', 'in_reply_to_screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'in_reply_to_status_id_str': '1885016457949413685', 'in_reply_to_user_id_str': '69156861', 'is_quote_status': False, 'lang': 'en', 'quote_count': 1, 'reply_count': 30, 'retweet_count': 1, 'retweeted': False, 'user_id_str': '1814030482478338048', 'id_str': '1885230004457337194'}}, '_legacy': {'bookmark_count': 8, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Fri Jan 31 07:33:53 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1885016457949413685', 'display_text_range': [14, 293], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '69156861', 'name': 'Chief Nerd', 'screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'indices': [0, 13]}]}, 'favorite_count': 60, 'favorited': False, 'full_text': '@TheChiefNerd Diagnosing autism was more difficult in the past. \n\nAutism was not well-known or understood until relatively recently. The term "autism" was first used in the 1940s by Hans Asperger and Leo Kanner, but it wasn\'t until much later that it gained wider recogn